</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Scrape</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/scrape</td><td class="px-3 py-2">1 / page</td></tr>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Crawl</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/crawl</td><td class="px-3 py-2">1 / page scraped</td></tr>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Map</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/map</td><td class="px-3 py-2">1 / call</td></tr>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Search</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/search</td><td class="px-3 py-2">2 / 10 results (rounded up)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Batch Scrape</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/batch/scrape</td><td class="px-3 py-2">1 / URL (sync + async modes)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Extract</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/extract</td><td class="px-3 py-2">1 / URL (LLM-driven)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Interact</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/interact</td><td class="px-3 py-2">2-7 / browser minute (1-min minimum)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Monitor</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/monitor</td><td class="px-3 py-2">1 / page / check</td></tr>
<tr><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>Agent</strong></td><td class="px-3 py-2">POST</td><td class="px-3 py-2">/v2/agent</td><td class="px-3 py-2">5 daily runs free, dynamic beyond</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">On top of the v2 endpoints, Firecrawl ships the JSON mode format spec for v2 /scrape: <code is:raw>ts: [{`{type: "json", schema: {type: "object", properties: {title: {type: "string"}}, required: ["title"]}}`}{`{type: "object", properties: {title: {type: "string"}}, required: ["title"]}`}}]</code>. The schema is OpenAI's tool-call JSON schema verbatim — no translation layer. Returned data satisfies the schema inline, eliminating the second LLM call that v1 forced. For the single-URL extract workflow (one URL, one schema, one call), JSON mode is the right tool. For anything beyond a single URL, Extract or Agent handle the multi-URL case.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The <strong>MCP server</strong> exposure is what makes Firecrawl unique among scraping tools. <code>npx -y firecrawl-mcp-server</code> registers Scrape / Search / Parse as native MCP tools in Claude Code / Cursor / Windsurf; the hosted MCP keyless endpoint at <code>https://api.firecrawl.dev/mcp/sse</code> exposes the same three tools without an API key (low rate limits, ideal for evaluation). When the agent needs to scrape the page it is currently looking at, the call goes through Firecrawl without leaving the agent loop. The official SKILL.md onboarding at <code>firecrawl.dev/agent-onboarding/SKILL.md</code> handles CLI / browser auth, API key setup, and a capability summary in a single file.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Firecrawl pricing: credits, not tokens</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Firecrawl is the only major scraping API that uses a credit-based plan system, not per-call metering at the per-token level. Every API call consumes a fixed number of credits (1 credit per Scrape / Crawl / Map / Batch URL / Monitor page, 2 credits per 10 Search results, 2-7 credits per Interact browser-minute, Agent free 5 times/day). Credits reset at the start of every billing cycle and do not roll over. Extra credits are sold in $5 packs (1k / 2k / 2.5k / 5k credits depending on plan).</p>

