<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Effective per-credit rates on the yearly plans: Hobby ~$0.0032, Standard ~$0.00083, Growth ~$0.000666, Scale ~$0.000599. Free is genuinely free — no credit card, 1,000 credits/month, and the Agent's 5 daily free runs apply even on Free. The Standard plan at $83/mo with 100k credits is the practical sweet spot for production RAG and crawl workloads; the Free plan is enough to validate a single-domain pipeline and decide whether the credit cost model fits your usage pattern.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Two features worth highlighting on the Enterprise tier: <strong>Zero-Data-Retention (ZDR) Search</strong>, which lets enterprise customers opt out of having search queries and result URLs logged for retention beyond the request lifetime; and <strong>SIEM audit logging</strong>, which streams a structured event to the customer's SIEM (starting with Microsoft Sentinel) for every scrape the team runs. IP restrictions (allowlist of CIDR ranges) and per-key endpoint / output-format restrictions are also enforced server-side on Enterprise — useful for compliance teams that need to lock an API key to specific output formats.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">How the Firecrawl API actually works</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The v2 API base URL is <code>https://api.firecrawl.dev/v2</code> with a <code>Bearer</code> token issued from the Firecrawl dashboard. Every endpoint accepts the same <code>Authorization</code> header and returns JSON. The SDKs (Python <code>pip install firecrawl</code>, Node <code>npm install firecrawl</code>) wrap the raw HTTP endpoints so most callers never touch <code>curl</code>.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Scrape (single URL) — the canonical call:</strong></p>

<pre class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 p-4 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto my-4"><code>// JavaScript / Node SDK
import Firecrawl from 'firecrawl-js';

const fc = new Firecrawl({ apiKey: process.env.FIRECRAWL_API_KEY });

const result = await fc.scrape('https://docs.firecrawl.dev', {
  formats: [
    { type: 'markdown' },
    { type: 'json', schema: {
      type: 'object',
      properties: {
        title: { type: 'string' },
        version: { type: 'string' }
      },
      required: ['title']
    } }
  ],
  maxAge: 3600000,           // accept cache up to 1 hour old
  onlyMainContent: true      // strip nav, footer, sidebar
});

console.log(result.data);    // { success: true, markdown: '...', json: { title: '...', version: '...' } }
</code></pre>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The <code>maxAge</code> parameter controls cache freshness — default 172800000 ms (2 days); cache hits return in milliseconds. <code>maxAge: 0</code> forces a fresh scrape; <code>storeInCache: false</code> returns the page but does not persist it. For RAG pipelines that re-scrape the same docs hourly, the cache is the difference between millisecond and multi-second response times.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Batch Scrape (sync mode):</strong></p>

<pre class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 p-4 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto my-4"><code>// Python SDK — sync batch
from firecrawl import Firecrawl

fc = Firecrawl(api_key='fc-...')

batch = fc.batch_scrape(
    urls=[
        'https://docs.firecrawl.dev/scrape',
        'https://docs.firecrawl.dev/crawl',
        'https://docs.firecrawl.dev/agent'
    ],
    formats=['markdown'],
    max_age=86400000  # 24h cache
)
print(batch.data)  # { status: 'completed', completed: 3, total: 3, data: [...] }
</code></pre>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Async mode accepts the same call but returns immediately with <code is:raw>{id, status: 'queued'}</code>; poll <code>/v2/batch/scrape/:id</code> or register a webhook for completion. All scrape options (formats, JSON schema, headers, maxAge, onlyMainContent) pass through to each URL. The default job timeout in the concurrency queue is 48 hours.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Interact (browser automation):</strong></p>

<pre class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 p-4 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto my-4"><code>// cURL — prompt-driven browser session
curl -X POST https://api.firecrawl.dev/v2/interact \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $FIRECRAWL_API_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "url": "https://example.com/login",
    "prompt": "click the login button, fill email@example.com into the email field, click submit, then scrape the resulting dashboard"
  }'
# Returns: { success, code, execution_logs, interactive_screenshot, captures: [...] }
# Cost: 7 credits / browser minute, 1-minute minimum
</code></pre>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Interact accepts either <code>code</code> (Playwright code, 2 credits/minute) or <code>prompt</code> (natural-language description, 7 credits/minute). Each session bills a 1-minute minimum even for sub-second probe runs. The session returns an <code>interactive_screenshot</code> plus the resulting <code>captures</code> array — useful for login-walled scraping, JS-rendered SPAs, end-to-end form testing.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Agent (autonomous multi-site research):</strong></p>

<pre class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 p-4 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto my-4"><code>// Node SDK — agent with JSON schema output
const result = await fc.agent({
  prompt: 'Find the current API pricing for the top 3 LLM providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google) and return as JSON',
  schema: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      providers: {
        type: 'array',
        items: {
          type: 'object',
          properties: {
            name: { type: 'string' },
            flagship_model: { type: 'string' },
            input_price: { type: 'string' },
            output_price: { type: 'string' }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
});

// 5 daily runs free on every plan (Free / Hobby / Standard / Growth / Scale)
// usage-based dynamic pricing beyond
</code></pre>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Agent composes with Extract for multi-URL structured research: use Agent to discover the URLs, then Extract with a JSON schema to pull structured fields. The 5-daily-free quota applies on every plan including Free, so evaluation workflows can run a useful multi-site research pipeline before committing to a paid tier.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Claude Code, Cursor, and Windsurf integration via MCP</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Firecrawl's hosted MCP server at <code>https://api.firecrawl.dev/mcp/sse</code> exposes Scrape / Search / Parse as native MCP tools. Three integration patterns:</p>

<ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-700 space-y-2 my-4">
<li><strong>Keyless MCP (low rate limits)</strong> — point Claude Code / Cursor at the hosted MCP endpoint without an API key. Scrape / Search / Parse work; rate limits are tightest.</li>
<li><strong>API-key MCP (full rate limits)</strong> — register the MCP server with <code>npx -y firecrawl-mcp-server</code> and supply <code>FIRECRAWL_API_KEY</code>. All endpoints and full rate limits.</li>
<li><strong>Self-hosted MCP</strong> — run the Firecrawl Docker image locally and point MCP at the local URL; eliminates transpacific latency for mainland China agents and removes the data-residency gap.</li>
</ol>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">When the agent needs to scrape the page it is currently looking at, the call goes through Firecrawl without leaving the agent loop — the MCP tool surface handles the auth, the retry-on-429, the maxAge cache, and the format conversion. This is the design point that differentiates Firecrawl from earlier scraping APIs: it is built for in-agent invocation, not for batch ETL pipelines run from a separate worker.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For agent platforms that can mint a WorkOS ID-JAG for Firecrawl (verified auth delegation tokens), the auth path is simpler: <code>https://www.firecrawl.dev/auth.md</code> describes the flow and the WorkOS ID-JAG minting endpoint. The official SKILL.md onboarding at <code>firecrawl.dev/agent-onboarding/SKILL.md</code> handles the standard CLI / browser auth path, the API key setup, and a capability summary in a single file.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Firecrawl vs Apify, Browse AI, Zyte, and the other scraping APIs</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The scraping API market in August 2026 has four meaningful categories. Where Firecrawl fits in:</p>

<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 space-y-2 my-4">
<li><strong>Direct HTML scraping (BeautifulSoup, Cheerio, Requests, Scrapy):</strong> free, fast on static sites, but breaks on every JS SPA, login wall, and bot-detection trigger. Firecrawl replaces this for any non-trivial site.</li>
<li><strong>Browser-automation-as-a-service (Browserbase, Steel, Anchor Browser):</strong> a managed Chromium that you drive with Playwright code. Lower-level than Firecrawl — you handle the scrape, parse, and extract pipeline yourself. Interact in Firecrawl overlaps with this category but bundles the parse / extract layer too.</li>
<li><strong>Headless scrapers (Apify, Browse AI, Zyte):</strong> stronger on structured data extraction, marketplace of prebuilt scrapers (Apify Actors), but more expensive per task and weaker on the multi-format JSON mode + MCP server story. Firecrawl's pricing is simpler (1 credit / page) and the JSON mode collapses the scrape + extract steps.</li>
<li><strong>Firecrawl:</strong> the first scraping API designed for in-agent invocation. Nine v2 endpoints under one credit system, JSON mode with OpenAI-format schema inline, MCP server with keyless Scrape / Search / Parse, self-hosted AGPL-3.0 Docker / Kubernetes for enterprises. Free tier with no credit card.</li>
</ul>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The honest frame for Firecrawl in August 2026: this is <strong>not</strong> a drop-in replacement for Apify if your team has built a large library of Apify Actors for specific sites. Firecrawl is the right tool when the scrape pipeline is generic (any URL → clean markdown / JSON) and the agent needs the call to happen from inside its tool loop. For teams that need both, the two APIs compose cleanly — Firecrawl for the generic pipeline and MCP-driven agent work, Apify for site-specific Actor libraries.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">China access, mainland China latency, and the regional story</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Firecrawl's hosted API runs from a single-region base URL at <code>https://api.firecrawl.dev</code> (US, San Francisco) with no documented Asia-Pacific or mainland China routing layer. North American and European production traffic sees single-digit tens of ms first-byte latency; mainland China requires a proxy or transit, with typical 200-400 ms transpacific first-byte latency on the hosted endpoint. The Free tier's 1,000 credits work behind a proxy for evaluation, but stable production traffic should either run a self-hosted Firecrawl deployment (Docker / Kubernetes) or sit behind a corporate proxy.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For enterprises with mainland China latency or data-residency requirements, the self-hosted AGPL-3.0 Docker / Kubernetes build at <code>github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl</code> runs entirely on the customer's infrastructure and removes the transpacific round trip. The build supports Bring-Your-Own-Database (Postgres / Redis) and Bring-Your-Own-Queue, so the entire pipeline — including the page cache and the Browserbase / Steel Interact backend — can run inside the customer's VPC. No ICP-licensed mainland China hosted endpoint as of August 2026, but the hosted MCP server at <code>api.firecrawl.dev/mcp/sse</code> serves Scrape / Search / Parse keyless endpoints globally at the lowest rate-limit tier for evaluation.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Who should use Firecrawl in 2026?</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Firecrawl fits cleanly into five engineering workflows in August 2026:</p>

<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 space-y-2 my-4">
<li><strong>RAG engineers</strong> who need to turn entire sites or large URL batches into clean markdown. The 2-day maxAge page cache and the JSON mode schema extraction collapse the most common RAG scrape + extract pipeline into one Firecrawl call.</li>
<li><strong>Agent teams building web-scale monitors</strong> for compliance, pricing, news, hiring, or product-page diffs. Monitor's three target types (Page / Website / Web-scale) cover the recurring-check use case end-to-end with webhook + email + Slack notifications.</li>
<li><strong>Product teams driving browsers via natural-language prompts</strong> for end-to-end web tests, form fills, or login-session interactions. Interact at 7 credits/minute (prompt-driven) or 2 credits/minute (Playwright code) handles the JS-rendered SPA and login-walled page cases that pure scrape cannot.</li>
<li><strong>Agent developers</strong> who want to scrape the current page from inside Claude Code / Cursor / Windsurf via <code>firecrawl-mcp-server</code>. The MCP integration is the design point that makes Firecrawl unique among scraping tools.</li>
<li><strong>Enterprises constrained by mainland China latency or compliance</strong> — self-host the Firecrawl Docker / Kubernetes image, keep all data inside the VPC, remove the transpacific round trip.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Verdict</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Firecrawl is the scraping layer for the agent era. Nine v2 endpoints behind one credit system, JSON mode that collapses the scrape + extract pipeline into one call, maxAge page cache for millisecond returns on cache hits, MCP server with keyless Scrape / Search / Parse for in-agent invocation, and a self-hosted AGPL-3.0 Docker / Kubernetes build for enterprises that need to run the entire stack on their own infrastructure. The pricing is genuinely competitive — Free with no credit card, Hobby at $19/mo, Standard at $83/mo for 100k credits — and the per-credit effective rates on the Standard plan (~$0.00083) put Firecrawl at the cheapest mainstream scraping API in the credit-based billing tier.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For teams building web-aware agents that need to scrape the current page from inside the agent loop, Firecrawl is the most direct single-vendor answer in August 2026. The MCP server integration is the design point that no other scraping API matches. For teams that have built large libraries of site-specific Apify Actors, Firecrawl is a complement rather than a replacement — use Firecrawl for the generic pipeline and MCP-driven agent work, Apify for site-specific Actor libraries. For enterprises that need mainland China latency or data residency, self-host the Docker / Kubernetes image and the transpacific round trip disappears.</p>
